  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4f/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_27.png",
  "plot_summary": "The FF are marveling at Reed's new thought-projector device, but Sue excuses herself before someone suggests she try it.&nbsp; Reed then admits to Ben and Johnny that he is about to go searching for an engagement ring.\n\nBut little does he know that \"at that very moment,\" the Sub-Mariner is lamenting that \"a prince must have a bride to <i>share</i> his throne!\" and \"If I could have the surface girl I love at my side, nothing else would matter!\"&nbsp; When he announces his intentions to his last few remaining loyal subjects, they disagree and then take off, commenting that, \"He left us no choice!&nbsp; We <i>must</i> desert him!&nbsp; We cannot be loyal to a monarch who would betray his royal heritage!\"&nbsp; So Namor is alone again - forced to follow his heart.\n\nAnd soon, the Sub-Mariner attacks!&nbsp; He quickly dispatches the Thing and Human Torch, then tells Sue why he has come.&nbsp; She warns him that Reed will find out (when he returns) and come after him, to which he replies \"Richards has had <i>years</i> to court you, to make you love him!&nbsp; All I ask is 24 hours, and I shall <i>have </i>them!!\"&nbsp; And he <i>gasses</i> her to sleep.&nbsp; As he soars off with her, he muses, \"...she <i>must</i> marry me!&nbsp; She <i>must</i>!&nbsp; Or else I have sacrificed everything in vain!\"\n\nReed soon returns with engagement ring in hand, only to learn from Ben and Johnny what has happened.&nbsp; Reed uncharacteristically flies off the handle, completely enraged, and snaps at them, \"Namor is <i>mine!</i>  He's stolen the one thing I love most in the world!&nbsp; ...<i>Nothing</i> will save him now!\"&nbsp; And Reed heads off to his \"radar-sweep-ray\" to find Namor, with Ben concluding that they've \"got to find a way to reach the Sub-Mariner <i>first</i>!\"\n\nBut when Ben points out they don't have \"any of the hocus-pocus stuff of Reed's to help us find him,\" it gives Johnny an idea of \"the <i>one</i> person who might be able to find Namor...the one man who <i>knows</i> hocus-pocus!\"&nbsp; And he sky-writes a message to \"Dr. Strange\" to contact him.\n\nAnd sure enough, in his Greenwich Village sanctuary, Dr. Strange takes note and sends his \"ectoplasmic form\" to the Baxter Building, where he sees the departure of Reed and then learns from Ben and Johnny about what's going on.&nbsp; He agrees to help find the Sub-Mariner and sends his shadowy form into the depths, soon locating \"a magnificent ornate castle.\"\n\nInside, Sue declares to Namor, \"Do you think you can make me confess I love you by <i>imprisoning</i> me?\"&nbsp; But Namor explains he's had no chance to date her the way Richards has.&nbsp; \"All I ask is 24 hours...\"&nbsp; Sue is having none of it and indicates that once Reed finds her, \"<i>nothing</i> can save you!\"\n\nDr. Strange has seen all and departs to tell Ben and Johnny, but notices Reed heading inside at the same time as his shadowy form withdraws.&nbsp; Reed attacks Namor, who declares \"<i>This</i> time there are no others to help you!&nbsp; <i>This</i> time you die!\"&nbsp; And as Reed and Namor battle, they are interrupted by the sudden return of a squad of undersea warriors, who declare, \"We realized we could <i>not</i> desert our prince and still be worthy of the name sub-mariners!\" Reed is now cornered, but he's determined to \"finish you off forever!\"\n\nMeanwhile, Dr. Strange returns to the Baxter Building to inform the Thing and Human Torch, and once he returns in his actual, physical body, he promises to \"send you to Prince Namor by the fastest way imaginable!\"&nbsp; And before they have time to blink, the pair are there with Reed, watching him be attacked by Namor's \"deep-sea stooges havin' target practice with our bouncin' buddy!\"&nbsp; And they join in on the fray.\n\nNamor is fine with them being there, as it will allow him to destroy all of them at once.&nbsp; But Reed still is enraged, saying, \"I'm going to make him wish he'd never <i>heard</i> of the human race!\"&nbsp; Namor is actually surprised to see Reed so passionate and thinks that perhaps he now finally understands his own feelings of losing his kingdom -- everything he holds dear... \"because of you accursed <i>humans</i>!\"&nbsp; But Reed doesn't care.&nbsp; \"So long as you live, mankind will be in danger!!&nbsp; I'll see to it that Namor the <i>First</i> becomes Namor the <i>Last</i>!\"\n\nBen handles the guards while Johnny flies off to free Sue, while meanwhile, Namor and Reed continue to battle it out.&nbsp; Reed's pliable body is able to withstand Namor's far stronger blows, until Namor finally realizes that \"We live by different codes!&nbsp; They've <i>never</i> understood me!&nbsp; They'll <i>always </i>hound me... unless I stop them now... stop them forever!\"&nbsp; He has his men aim their undersea blast gun at Reed and Ben and offers them the \"privileges of prisoners of war\" if they surrender unconditionally.&nbsp; Of course they refuse, and Namor unleashes the gun, but it mysteriously does no harm!&nbsp; Sue's invisible force field has protected them!\n\nNamor challenges her on the spot to choose between himself and Reed, only to hear Sue state for the record that, \"Reed is the only man for me... nothing can ever change that!\"&nbsp; This sends Namor into an outrage, but just as he prepares to strike, an onlooking Dr. Strange sees the major escalation that is about to take place, sees that \"the sea prince has never been so scorned before!&nbsp; His rage is mounting!&nbsp; He is capable of <i>anything</i>!\"&nbsp; He transports the FF to safety, even as Namor gives the command to seize them!\n\nReed is still unsure that Sue actually <i>meant</i> what she said about loving him.&nbsp; Did she <i>mean </i>it, or did she just say that to prevent any further fighting?\n\nBut we see Sue's thoughts, \"Oh, Reed, you blind fool!&nbsp; Of <i>course</i> it's you I love!!&nbsp; But how can I ever <i>convince</i> you?&nbsp; You'll always suspect I may have just been trying to protect you... or Namor!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "An overcrowded story leaves characterization struggling to keep up.\nLogic occasionally gasps for air, but there's energy to carry it across the finish line.",
  "subtitle": "Overconfidence, Overreaction, and One Strange Guest Star",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | PopTheQuestion | width=15%] Though the story starts off by showing that Reed is finally getting serious about his relationship with Sue, it quickly transitions over to the Sub-Mariner, making readers wonder whether this story was a natural progression from the scene we saw from Sue a couple of issues ago (in [LINK:1964:FF_25|FF #25|2027-05-10], where she finally realized how deeply she felt about Reed), <b>Or</b>... was this showdown between Reed and Namor planned all along, and this was just the first opportunity to act on it?\n\nWe don't know the answer, but for whatever reason, the issue seems bound and determined to stay focused on these two people (or <i>mostly </i>so)...\n\nWhile it's true that Namor's last appearance in <i>this</i> magazine was in last summer's annual ([LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1]), it's not as if readers hadn't had a more recent peek at some of his activities.&nbsp; Remember that he joined up with the Hulk in [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01] (back in November), which ultimately led to him freeing Captain America in that title's following issue (the legendary [LINK:1964:AVEN_4|AVEN #4|2027-04-25], published only a couple of months ago), where we also learned that he <i>did</i> still have a handful of warrior troops who remained loyal to him.&nbsp; They even returned to the ocean with the Sub-Mariner at the end of that adventure, believing (erroneously) that the Avengers were finished.\n\nSo it comes as no surprise that when he finally appears in <i>this</i> issue, he mentions that he's still <i>mostly</i> alone, only having \"a few faithful warriors\" that remain at his side.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=(And by the way, kudos to Stan and Jack for the accurate follow-through on the continuity here.)]\n\n[ASSET: left | PrincessSue | width=20%] [ASSET: right | AbandonedAgain | width=17%] Yet, when the narrative joins him on page 3, he has decided to try once again to make Sue Storm his princess because he feels that having a princess by his side is the only way a ruler like him can eventually reunite his undersea empire and cement himself as \"Prince Namor.\"&nbsp; (And in this, he is obviously misguided, <i>because</i>, as the annual showed, it was his feelings for the \"surface girl\" that Lady Dorma and the rest of the Atlanteans abandoned him in the <i>first place</i>.)\n[BR]\n[BR] The moment he announces this plan to his few remaining subjects, they promptly take off, proving that Namor simply hasn't learned his lesson!&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But also freeing him up to battle the FF once more.&nbsp; It <i>is</i> the FF's magazine after all, and that's apparently all that matters!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | Claiming24hours | width=13%] The Sub-Mariner's arrival at the Baxter Building, battle with the Thing and the Human Torch, followed by his subsequent kidnapping of Sue, all while telling her that Reed Richards has <i>had</i> his chance to court her...\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=The <i>audacity</i> of it all actually made me laugh!]\n[BR]\n[BR] Oh <i>sure</i>!&nbsp; Gas the girl to sleep, haul her off, and demand that she confess her undying love toward him!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Hahahaha!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Not much on romance, are you?&nbsp; <i>Geez!</i>)]\n\nThis only shows how <i>little</i> Namor has learned since he first laid eyes on Sue back in [LINK:1962:FF_4|FF #4] (published over two years ago), where he offered to \"show mercy to the rest of your pitiful race\" if she would only be his bride.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Failing that, <i>clearly</i> the next step in his mind was to resort to kidnapping!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=italic; text=Holy plot acceleration, Batman!]\n[BR] (And yet, it's all done with a straight <i>face</i>, no less!&nbsp; You almost can't make this up!)\n\nIt never ceases to <i>amaze</i> me that Namor is so arrogant that he believes he can kidnap a woman and take only 24 hours to sway her into falling in love with him and agreeing to be his bride.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=This plot was so hurriedly thrust on the reader that it barrels forward at breakneck speed.]\n\nIt takes only one page from Subby's initial appearance in the story before he storms into the Baxter Building and kidnaps his intended princess!&nbsp; (And it makes me wonder if suddenly-confused readers didn't actually page back in the story, thinking \"Did I <i>miss</i> something?\")\n\nOnce again, when it comes to Namor:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=Subtlety Sold Separately!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I <i>know</i> there are a limited number of pages, but <i>geez</i>!&nbsp; I think another page or two devoted to setup might have been <i>okay</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<i>Naturally</i>, it's all done while Reed is off buying the engagement ring, which is simply just the latest episode of [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Comic Book Convenience 101]!\n\nReed's out-of-control rage was quite eye-opening.&nbsp; I understand that he was livid because of the audacious Namor thinking he could steal his girl away from him, just at the very moment he was about to give her the ring, but his reaction -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=his declaration that he was going to finish him off for <i>good</i>]... <i>well</i>... we can only hope his great intelligence would have kicked in at the final moment and prevented him from striking the fatal blow... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=but <i>boy</i> was he ever pissed!]\n\nOff he goes, leaving his teammates behind, which finally brings us to the promised guest star...\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThe appearance of Doctor Strange was fine, with the exception of how he was initially summoned.&nbsp; Johnny sent out the request for help with only the hint that a \"Dr. Strange\" even existed.&nbsp; Readers can only assume that Johnny knew of him because they share a monthly magazine, namely [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Strange Tales] [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=(proving that Marvel isn't above winking directly at its audience)].\n[ASSET: center | CallingDrStrange | width=large]\n\nThe guest star had very little to do, but I think that was the point.&nbsp; He was presumably only there to boost sales.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=Actually, he had one <i>more</i> use, which was to magically transport the FF away from the enraged Namor when the page count ran out.]\n\n[ASSET: left | AnotherWayToHelp | width=18%] [ASSET: right | SpecialPill | width=12%] To Stan's credit, he <i>at least</i> has Dr. Strange ponder how different it is to be involved with the more mundane ways of helping mankind.&nbsp; At the very least, by agreeing to help the Fantastic Four, Strange shows that he does not condone Sue's kidnapping.\n[BR]\n[BR] And yet, when he spots Reed about to storm in on Namor, I suspect he doesn't condone that <i>either</i>!\n\nHaving Dr. Strange on hand to whisk them away to safety ensured the content would fit into a one-issue story instead of it extending into the next issue (which would also have been okay -- and might have helped with some of the very-rushed characterization).\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | IfOnlyICouldBreakFree | width=14%] Page 12 shows Sue watching Reed and Namor trade blows, but she thinks,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"If only I could break free before they kill each other!\"]\n[BR]\n[BR] I confess I had to scratch my head here, wanting to point out,\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=\"Sue, you don't <i>need</i> to break free!&nbsp; Just use your force field power to intervene!\"]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But both Stan and Jack seem to have forgotten that -- she helps only after Johnny frees her on page 16.)]\n\nPages 14-21 were pretty much all-out war, which I'm sure suited Jack Kirby just fine, and for once, Reed exhibits some skill on the battlefield (not to mention some serious rage):\n[ASSET: center | ReedIsPissed | width=large]\n\nOnce Ben and Johnny joined the battle, I liked how the Thing was juggling the other Atlanteans on page 18 (they conveniently returned, of course, having realized their error) and looked very much like he (Ben) desired nothing more than some popcorn to eat while he watched Reed and Namor duke it out.\n\nI'm sure it must have been <i>quite</i> gratifying for him to watch the FF's leader lose control that way, especially since <i>he</i> is the one the rest of the team normally has to hold back.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=Very nice.]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | PowerOfLove | width=11%] With the page count dwindling, Namor finally gave Sue his ultimatum, demanding that she choose between him and the accursed Reed Richards.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=And it doesn't take a genius to know who Sue actually prefers.]\n[BR]\n[BR] Thank goodness Dr. Strange was still watching!&nbsp; He whisks them to safety with only half a page to spare, allowing the issue to end.\n\n[ASSET: right | RoughArtwork | width=9%] As for the artwork in this issue, I found it [SPAN: severity=error; text=very sub-par,] particularly with the images of the Thing, whose look appeared more rough, less detailed.&nbsp; It might have been the inking of \"Geo. Bell,\" but there's no telling.&nbsp; Reed and the Thing in <i>particular</i> looked far less defined and hurried, and with a scene-stealing presence such as the Thing, that's probably something they should get right.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; text=I found it distracting.]\n\nFinally, [SPAN: severity=warning; text=what's with the Sub-Mariner's red swimming trunks on the cover?]&nbsp; His trunks have always been green before, and this is the only place in the magazine where they aren't!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=Oops (perhaps)?]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nIt's difficult to read this story without bursting out laughing, especially in the opening moments:\n[HANG] * first because of Namor's absolute <b><i>cluelessness</i></b><i> </i>about how to woo a woman,\n[HANG] * to the abject <b><i>overreaction</i></b> of Reed Richards,\n[HANG] * to the <b><i>meta-awareness</i></b> of the Human Torch about his solo magazine's co-star.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=This was clearly a case where there was simply too much plot to cram into a single book.]\n\nAdding Dr. Strange to the mix only exacerbated the problem.&nbsp; I think the co-creators could have found a way to tell the story without him (but I'm guessing Stan felt it was high time for him to appear somewhere outside of his lonely corner of the Marvel Universe).&nbsp; The spectacle simply wasn't worth it in a month where there was already too much story to tell -- it ended up sacrificing needed characterization that could have better served the story.\n\nI also wonder if the story could have been better had Stan and Jack let it unfold more organically.&nbsp; You'd <i>just completed</i> a two-parter in the last couple of issues, showing that they <i>could</i> take their time, but my guess is that Stan hadn't yet heard back from readers as to whether they actually <i>liked</i> the idea of the two-part story, and so he decided to finish this one off in a single issue.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=Fair enough -- so <i>do</i> that and perhaps wait on Dr. Strange for <i>next </i>time!]\n[BR] (This story might have had more impact had it been allowed to breathe a little more.)\n\nSpeaking of <i>breathing</i>, I was a <i>little</i> annoyed that first Reed, then Ben and Johnny, had <i>extremely convenient</i> \"underwater breathin' pills\" at their disposal that allowed them to, according to Reed, \"do without oxygen for one full hour!\"&nbsp; The existence of such pills would seem an eminently saleable invention and thus completely eliminate all of the Fantastic Four's financial problems, likely <b><i>forever</i>!</b>\n\nBut does Reed share this technology with the general public?&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Heck no!]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But... <i>hey</i>... it's a comic book after all!)]\n\nSome other problems I had with the story were that I felt it pushed the limits of Reed's personality to near breaking point.&nbsp; I've already spoken on this above, but more importantly, I think this issue diminished the character of the Sub-Mariner.\n\nUp until now, Namor's greatest strength as a character has been that readers could at least understand <i>why</i> he behaved the way he did.&nbsp; But in one issue, Namor went from a near-sympathetic figure to a kidnapper, one who appeared ready to stop his enemies <b>forever</b>.&nbsp; He may call that \"living by a different code\" (as he indicates on page 21), but that, along with his page 11 declaration to Reed that \"This time you die!\"\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=<i>Well</i>... let's just say I'm not as sympathetic anymore -- that's Dr. Doom-level talk, if you ask me!]\n\nThe real irony of Namor's plan is that [LINK:1963:FF_Annual_1|FF Annual 1] had already demonstrated exactly why it could never work.&nbsp; His fixation on Sue is the reason Atlantis turned against him in the first place.&nbsp; Far from helping to reunite his kingdom, pursuing her again immediately costs him what little support he still has.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Namor spends this entire issue trying to solve a problem by doubling down on the thing that caused it.]\n\nDespite a host of problems, rushed pace, questionable artwork, the book actually made me laugh, likely <i>because</i> of its breakneck pace.&nbsp; I didn't think the guest star added much to the actual story beyond providing a convenient exit once the fighting was over, but Namor's cockamamie scheme to win a girl by kidnapping her, Reed's over-the-top reaction, Johnny's <i>wink-wink-nod-nod</i> at the audience, and the underwater free-for-all was entertaining enough to earn a mid-grade rating in the end.\n\n[HANG0] [SPAN: severity=info; text=Though I enjoyed it (and had fun reading it), I still feel that space wasn't the only thing sacrificed...]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Namor's character was short-changed as well.]",
  "side_note": [
    "This is, of course, the very first crossover story involving Dr. Strange.&nbsp; Up until now, he has been quietly occupying his own corner of the Marvel Universe (something that I've actually commented on).&nbsp; While it's nice to know he isn't on an island by himself, I found his use here far more perfunctory, as if done <i>only</i> to reinforce to readers that he actually <i>does</i> co-exist with the other Marvel characters.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=That's <i>something</i>, but... was it enough?]"
  ],
  "letters_page": "Gregory Suriano says \"I have to congratulate you on those witty little 'remarks' you sometimes insert into the boxes with the names of the artists, writer and letterer.\"&nbsp; <i>True enough</i>!&nbsp; I agree that those small things always help make <i>me </i>smile as well!&nbsp; Goofy?&nbsp; <i>Sure</i>!&nbsp; But fun as well.\n\nJim Grondin's letter applauds Marvel for its various magazines and ends his letter with \"I also think that you are very conceited!\" -- to which the editor replies:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=Jim, we can't understand why people seem to think that we talented Marvel geniuses are conceited!]&nbsp; (<i>Heh</i>, you gotta love it!)",
  "highlight": "Namor's kidnapping scheme, Reed's overly volcanic temper, and a mostly decorative Dr. Strange make for a rushed but entertaining showdown."
}